COMPLEX MODULARITY

================================================================================

In the current compiler, levels are represented in the block stack, and the 
fixed symbol table.

In complex modularity, we add the module as a level. Each module has its own
block stack and symbol table. There is both a linear module list for all
modules processed in the system, active or not, and a stack that represents
the current nesting level of modules. When a symbol is looked for, the search,
if global, proceeds though any number of module levels to find it. The work
is minimized by using the same hash to move from module to module, and the
search is that of a single list as interrupted by the module system.

The first module created is the system module. This sits at the bottom of
the module stack for the run of the program. Next, any uses modules are
created. As each module is parsed, a new module level is opened. When a
module opens a submodule, that is either stacked anew, and the module
parsed into that, or a previous module is retrived from the list and
set active. Because each module dictates its own ordering, modules must
be stacked up and torn down at will, which is why having them as self
contained units is a good idea.

When a symbol is processed, it goes through the module stack, as mentioned.
We also have a "qualident" format of:

out.str

Where the module of origin is directly specified. This then searches through
the module list to find the appropriate entry directly.

The intermediate file format changes for complex modularity. Instead of
having the system symbols as another, outter block, each module in the list
is output to the file, without regard for order, except that the client
module is output last. Each module is introduced by a card that gives the
module number, 1 to N, in the file. Each intermediate link then specifies
the module number as well as the level and sequence of its target.

As now, each intermediate completely specifies its modules used, although
the change would make it easier to move to a distributed format. When
the parser wants to use a file for its precompilation, it loads the
modules in the file into the module list, ending up with the used module
itself.

